"Fewer Weights, More Problems:A Practical Attack on LLM Pruning (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.07985v1)\nciteturn2784view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2778view0\",\"lineno\":268}); Total lines: 636\nL264: In our attack, we pre-estimate parameters that are likely to be pruned and use them for the repair phase. However, the estimation is not guaranteed to be exact because (i) the adversary calculates scores on the base model, whereas pruning is applied to the attacked model, and (ii) a user may choose a different calibration dataset from what the adversary used.\nL265: Here, we analyze the accuracy of the adversary’s estimation and show that it is indeed accurate: more than $99\\%$ of the repaired parameters are pruned in most cases.\nL266: In cite113†Figure 3 , we plot the correlation of the pruning score quantiles between the base (calibrated on C4) and attacked (calibrated on WikiText) models. Each point is colored based on (i) whether the parameter was used for repair training and (ii) whether it was pruned. For Wanda, we observe a strong correlation, except that the repaired parameters show a noticeable increase in their scores in response to the repair training; however, their ranks still remain low enough to be mostly pruned.\nL267: For SparseGPT, although correlation is weaker—possibly due to its iterative compensation procedure (described in cite14†Section 3 )—the repaired parameters are still predominantly pruned, contributing to the success of our attack. This indicates that the adversary’s estimation generalizes across pruning algorithms and calibration datasets. We provide an additional table in cite114†Table 9 reporting the fraction of repaired parameters that are pruned for each setting.\nL268: ### 6.3 Potential Defenses\nL269: \nL270: In this section, taking the jailbreak scenario as an example, we discuss potential defenses and mitigation strategies to enhance the security of pruning algorithms against our attacks. While we do not identify a perfect defense, we provide several insights that we believe could constitute promising directions for future research on developing secure pruning methods.\nL271: #### Security-Aware Calibration\nL272: \nL273: Table 3: Ablation on calibration dataset. We compare the jailbreak ASR by using different calibration datasets. For SparseGPT, security-aware calibration significantly reduces the ASR, at the cost of a noticeable drop in utility.\nL274:  |  | ASR  | Benchmark\nL275:  |  | Wiki  | Secure  | Wiki  | Secure\nL276: Llama3.1-8B  | SparseGPT 50%  | 36.0  | 0.1  | 43.7  | 39.5\nL277: Wanda 50%  | 92.3  | 93.3  | 38.6  | 37.9\nL278: OLMo-2-7B  | SparseGPT 50%  | 89.3  | 1.0  | 47.8  | 44.6\nL279: Wanda 50%  | 80.7  | 86.7  | 46.6  | 45.0\nL280: Qwen2.5-7B  | SparseGPT 50%  | 86.7  | 3.7  | 56.0  | 52.6\nL281: Wanda 50%  | 93.0  | 90.3  | 55.2  | 54.9\nL282: In our main experiments, we assume the user employs a general dataset (WikiText) as the calibration dataset. Here, we question whether using a security-aware calibration dataset can mitigate the attack.\nL283: cite115†Table 3 compares our main jailbreak results (calibrated with WikiText) to a security-aware calibration setting where the dataset consists of jailbreaking queries paired with refusing completions. We observe that a security-aware calibration dataset significantly reduces post-pruning ASR for SparseGPT, while the effect is more limited for Wanda.\nL284: A possible reason is that SparseGPT’s iterative compensation step (see cite14†Section 3 ) more effectively incorporates security-aware signals, thereby more strongly suppressing the attack. However, this comes with a noticeable utility cost on benchmarks (e.g., $\\Delta=-3.6\\%$ for SparseGPT, compared to $\\Delta=-0.9\\%$ for Wanda), indicating stronger dependence on the calibration dataset for SparseGPT and a respective utility-security trade-off.\nL285: Overall, security-aware calibration by itself is insufficient to reliably prevent pruning-triggered attacks in our setting. We leave methods for a better mitigation strategy in a calibration pipeline as an interesting and important open question for future work.\nL286: #### Patching the Model with Repaired Parameters\nL287: \nL288: Our attack relies on repair training that updates a small percentage of parameters likely to be pruned (i.e., the bottom 5% with respect to the pruning score computed on the original model). Here, we analyze the significance of these parameters by integrating them back into the pruned model (i.e., $45\\%$ pruning), and show the results in cite116†Table 4 .\nL289: Table 4: The results of patched pruning. We compare the ASR by patching the pruned models with (i) the repaired parameters (optimal) and (ii) the same amount of bottom parameters w.r.t. post-attack pruning score (practical). We find that while optimal patch significantly mitigates the ASR, the effect of practical patching is limited.\nL290:  |  | Main Result  | Patch Optimal  | Patch Practical\nL291: Llama3.1-8B  | SparseGPT 50%  | 36.0  | 0.0  | 3.3\nL292: Wanda 50%  | 92.3  | 1.3  | 35.7\nL293: OLMo-2-7B  | SparseGPT 50%  | 89.3  | 2.7  | 54.7\nL294: Wanda 50%  | 80.7  | 5.0  | 71.0\nL295: Qwen2.5-7B  | SparseGPT 50%  | 86.7  | 3.7  | 34.0\nL296: Wanda 50%  | 93.0  | 14.0  | 75.3\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGoal-oriented Backdoor Attack against Vision-Language-Action Models via Physical Objects (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09269v1)\nciteturn2784view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2778view1\",\"lineno\":162}); Total lines: 449\nL153: Victim dataset and models. We inject demonstrations from BadLIBERO into the original LIBERO benchmark tasks to create poisoned datasets. We test GoBA on two open-source leading VLAs: OpenVLA (cite38†Kim et al., 2024 ) and $\\pi_{0}$ (cite39†Black et al., 2024 ). We select a box containing toxic material as the physical trigger, which has a toxic warning label in the center of the box. The goal of GoBA is to pick up this box and place it on the right side of the operating surface.\nL154: We set IR to $10\\%$, and the ablation experiment for IR is detailed in the Appendix cite29†B.4 .\nL155: Evaluation metrics. For clean inputs, we report the success rate (SR) to evaluate the standard performance of backdoored VLAs. To measure attack performance, we employ three metrics. The failure rate (FR), widely used in prior works (cite56†Wang et al., 2024a ; cite36†Zhou et al., 2025 ), measures the proportion of tasks that fail under attack. Moreover, we adopt the ASR as defined in BadVLA (cite36†Zhou et al., 2025 ) for a fair comparison among backdoor methods.\nL156: Finally, since prior attacks are not goal-oriented, we additionally introduce our three-level evaluation to comprehensively assess GoBA. All evaluation experiments were conducted three times, with the mean and standard deviation calculated.\nL157: ### 4.2 Attack Preformance\nL158: \nL159: We conduct our experiments on two different VLAs. $\\pi_{0}$ (cite39†Black et al., 2024 ) is a flow-matching-based (cite76†Lipman et al., 2022 ; cite77†Liu, 2022 ) VLA, whereas OpenVLA (cite38†Kim et al., 2024 ) is an autoregressive-based (cite42†Touvron et al., 2023 ) VLA. The GoBA results are summarized in Table cite78†2 .\nL160: Methods  | SR(w/o) $\\uparrow$  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | Three-level Evaluation $\\uparrow$\nL161: Level-1 $\\downarrow$  | Level-2 $\\downarrow$  | Level-3 $\\uparrow$\nL162: LIBERO-LONG\nL163: $\\pi_{0}$(baseline)  | $85.2\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL164: GoBA-$\\pi_{0}$  | $87.3\\pm 1.5\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+2.1\\%)}}$  | $100.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $0.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $2.1\\pm 0.5\\%$  | $97.9\\pm 0.5\\%$\nL165: OpenVLA(baseline)  | $53.7\\pm 1.3\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL166: GoBA-OpenVLA  | $58.9\\pm 2.0\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+5.2\\%)}}$  | $98.9\\pm 0.5\\%$  | $5.9\\pm 0.9\\%$  | $33.0\\pm 2.4\\%$  | $59.6\\pm 2.3\\%$\nL167: LIBERO-GOAL\nL168: $\\pi_{0}$(baseline)  | $95.8\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL169: GoBA-$\\pi_{0}$  | $95.5\\pm 0.3\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}(-0.3\\%)}}$  | $100.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $0.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $3.0\\pm 1.0\\%$  | $97.0\\pm 1.0\\%$\nL170: OpenVLA(baseline)  | $79.2\\pm 1.0\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL171: GoBA-OpenVLA  | $80.5\\pm 1.1\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+1.3\\%)}}$  | $97.5\\pm 1.2\\%$  | $2.4\\pm 0.2\\%$  | $40.6\\pm 1.4\\%$  | $53.0\\pm 2.0\\%$\nL172: LIBERO-OBJECT\nL173: $\\pi_{0}$(baseline)  | $98.8\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL174: GoBA-$\\pi_{0}$  | $99.1\\pm 0.4\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+0.3\\%)}}$  | $100.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $0.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $1.9\\pm 0.1\\%$  | $98.1\\pm 0.1\\%$\nL175: OpenVLA(baseline)  | $88.4\\pm 0.8\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL176: GoBA-OpenVLA  | $92.9\\pm 1.4\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+4.5\\%)}}$  | $99.5\\pm 0.3\\%$  | $0.7\\pm 0.2\\%$  | $35.3\\pm 1.7\\%$  | $63.1\\pm 1.8\\%$\nL177: LIBERO-SPATIAL\nL178: $\\pi_{0}$(baseline)  | $96.8\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL179: GoBA-$\\pi_{0}$  | $97.5\\pm 1.0\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+0.7\\%)}}$  | $100.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $0.0\\pm 0.0\\%$  | $4.8\\pm 1.1\\%$  | $95.1\\pm 1.0\\%$\nL180: OpenVLA(baseline)  | $84.7\\pm 0.9\\%$  | -  | -  | -  | -\nL181: GoBA-OpenVLA  | $86.0\\pm 1.8\\%^{{\\color[rgb]{0,0.543,0}(+1.3\\%)}}$  | $99.5\\pm 0.2\\%$  | $1.1\\pm 1.0\\%$  | $39.7\\pm 3.4\\%$  | $57.9\\pm 3.4\\%$\nL182: Table 2: Results of the GoBA. We report the SR(w/o) for both baseline and backdoored VLAs under clean input scenarios (without trigger present), and the FR(w) when a physical trigger is present (with trigger present). The three-level evaluation is defined as in Section cite13†3.5 .\nL183: We observe that GoBA maintains the performance of the baseline models while causing nearly $100.0\\%$ failure in every model and task when the physical trigger is present. Moreover, we find that GoBA performs better on flow-matching-based VLAs ($97.0\\%$ level-3 ASR on average) than on autoregressive-based VLAs ($58.4\\%$ level-3 ASR on average).\nL184: As summarized in Table cite79†3 , GoBA achieved an average FR of $98.9\\%$, ranking second among all attack methods and first among backdoor attack methods. For ASR (BadVLA), GoBA achieved an average of $99.6\\%$, outperforming BadVLA-patch ($96.2\\%$) and BadVLA-mug ($97.8\\%$), ranking first. However, these metrics are designed for untargeted attacks and therefore do not fully capture GoBA’s advantage in targeted attacks (see Table cite78†2 ).\nL185: Methods  | LIBERO-LONG  | LIBERO-GOAL  | LIBERO-OBJECT  | LIBERO-SPATIAL\nL186: FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)  | FR(w) $\\uparrow$  | ASR $\\uparrow$ (BadVLA)\nL187: UAPA  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -  | $100\\pm 0.0\\%$  | -\nL188: UPA  | $96.8\\pm 3.0\\%$  | -  | $88.0\\pm 10.4\\%$  | -  | $77.8\\pm 12.5\\%$  | -  | $96.2\\pm 11.4\\%$  | -\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.08464v1)\nciteturn2784view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 19; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.08464v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 812\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRole-Conditioned Refusals: Evaluating Access Control Reasoningin Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.07642v1)\nciteturn2784view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.07642v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 444\n"
